From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Adam Hillson <598804+BadAd84@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 09:54:53 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 16/28] flashnext: the prompt tile runs two stages and two
 blocks an SM under TF_FLASHNEXT_PROMPT_GEMM2=1

The same tile and arithmetic with two pipeline stages instead of three and two blocks an SM
instead of one: one block output stores and pipeline fill overlap the other block K loop. The
per-K accumulation order and every block output are unchanged, so the bits are the 3-stage tile
bits; the two-stage stage bytes let both blocks share an SM on sm_120 and sm_121.
---
 CHANGELOG.md                               |  2 ++
 zig/kernels/cuda/qmmf.cu                   | 11 +++++++----
 zig/src/cuda/qmmf.zig                      | 23 ++++++++++++++++++----
 zig/src/families/flashnext/cuda_knobs.zig  | 10 ++++++++++
 zig/src/families/flashnext/cuda_prompt.zig |  1 +
 zig/src/platform/environment.zig           |  1 +
 zig/src/platform/environment_test.zig      |  1 +
 7 files changed, 41 insertions(+), 8 deletions(-)

diff --git a/CHANGELOG.md b/CHANGELOG.md
index d4164037e..a914eb490 100644
--- a/CHANGELOG.md
+++ b/CHANGELOG.md
@@ -83,6 +83,8 @@ Each release's page on GitHub has its notes and measurements. See [the 1.0.0 rel
 
 - Tests: `zig build sessions-writes` drives the real DeepSeek-V4.1 session store and NVMe index with synthetic agent, chat and mixed loads, no model or GPU, and reports the bytes an enabled tier (`TF_DSV41_SESSION_DISK`) writes and reads back; a nightly Linux test checks its accounting against real files (#2660).
 
+- Flash Next on CUDA: the relaxed 128 x 128 prompt tile also runs as a two-stage, two-blocks-an-SM variant (`TF_FLASHNEXT_PROMPT_GEMM2=1`) with the same arithmetic and bits: one block's output stores and pipeline fill overlap the other block's K loop (#9999).
+
 - Server: Responses grows each output item in one buffer, and Kimi chat advances its native stream parser through new tokens, so a long reply retains memory in proportion to its length while every reply and streamed event stays the same (#2642).
 - Tests: `zig build bench-stream-scan` streams a 32k-token thinking reply (30,000 reasoning tokens, prose, then a Qwen XML, GLM, DSML or Kimi call) through the chat, Messages and Responses routes one or two tokens a chunk, and prints each chunk's request-thread time at 1k to 30k reply tokens, each whole-text step's time and the request arena; under `test-server-cpu` a 300-token reply checks that each route's streamed reasoning, prose and call arguments equal the reply's (#2642).
 
diff --git a/zig/kernels/cuda/qmmf.cu b/zig/kernels/cuda/qmmf.cu
index 1a4aac801..f58c28b1f 100644
--- a/zig/kernels/cuda/qmmf.cu
+++ b/zig/kernels/cuda/qmmf.cu
@@ -369,8 +369,8 @@ __device__ __forceinline__ void prompt_mx_body(
